The **Agile Manifesto (2001)** values:
- individuals and interactions over processes and tools;
- working software over comprehensive documentation;
- customer collaboration over contract negotiation;
- responding to change over following a plan.

'Over' means the items on the right still have value — just less than those on the left.

Winning the deal is the start. Strong account managers:
- ensure successful onboarding and measurable outcomes;
- hold regular business reviews with metrics;
- build relationships across levels (users, managers, executives);
- spot expansion opportunities only when value is proven;
- address problems quickly and honestly.

Retaining and growing existing customers is often more efficient than constantly finding new ones.

The Act includes any one or more **unwelcome** acts or behaviour, such as:
- physical contact and advances;
- a demand or request for sexual favours;
- sexually coloured remarks;
- showing pornography;
- any other unwelcome physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature.

It also recognises circumstances such as implied or explicit promises of preferential treatment, threats about employment, interfering with work or creating an intimidating, offensive or hostile environment, and humiliating treatment likely to affect health or safety.

The key test is **'unwelcome'** — the impact on the recipient, not the intent ('just joking').

An Internal Committee includes:
- a **Presiding Officer** — a woman employed at a senior level;
- at least **two members** from among employees, preferably committed to the cause of women or with social-work experience or legal knowledge;
- **one external member** from an NGO or association committed to the cause of women, or a person familiar with issues relating to sexual harassment.

At least **half of the members must be women**.

The World Health Organization's ICD-11 describes burnout as an **occupational phenomenon** (not classified as a medical condition) resulting from **chronic workplace stress that has not been successfully managed**. It has three dimensions:
- **Exhaustion** — feelings of energy depletion;
- **Mental distance from one's job** — cynicism or negativity;
- **Reduced professional efficacy.**

Burnout overlaps with conditions like depression and anxiety; a doctor or mental-health professional can help tell them apart.

Skip-level meetings (you with your managers' reports) help you:
- hear the ground truth about priorities, morale and blockers;
- spot talent early;
- check whether strategy is understood;
- detect management problems that won't surface through layers.

They are **not** a channel to bypass or second-guess managers.

Before a big cross-functional decision meeting, Rashmi:
- met each stakeholder 1:1 to hear concerns;
- adjusted the proposal (added a rollback plan for operations; data-residency note for legal);
- asked the finance partner to present the cost slide herself (ownership);
- sent a one-page pre-read.

In the meeting, there were no surprises. 'Pre-wiring' is normal in many big companies — the meeting confirms what was built through conversations.

Harish's team imagined the festive launch failed. Reasons listed in 10 minutes:
- payment gateway overload at peak;
- vernacular translations confusing;
- support team untrained;
- competitor price drop.

Mitigations: load test at 3× peak, user-test translations with 20 merchants, a 2-hour support training, a pricing response plan. Total effort: two days.

Say a firm no — and seek guidance from your manager or compliance — if an elder or senior asks you to:
- share your login or password, or use theirs;
- backdate, alter or approve documents you haven't verified;
- process personal work on company time or systems;
- do something that conflicts with policy or law.

Age or seniority never makes these acceptable.
